**Hard exclusion — applies to every component diagram.** Never represent Dockerfiles, docker-compose files, CI/CD pipeline stages, Makefiles, IaC templates, configuration files, settings, environment variable declarations, framework wiring, build scripts, or other build/deployment/support tooling as nodes. This also means that a repository or directory containing no implemented components must produce no component diagram, rather than substituting a diagram of configuration or infrastructure relationships.

If this contains source code (real classes, modules, services, or packages), the diagram must depict only those implemented components and how they call, extend, or depend on one another — never mix in project files or any tooling listed above as nodes alongside the real code, even if they were discussed in the text above. Never include test files, test suites, mocks, fixtures, or testing frameworks as nodes — the diagram depicts the software's actual functionality, never how it is verified. Prioritize the codebase's own hand-written components; a third-party library or framework class should appear as a node only when it is genuinely central to the functionality being diagrammed (e.g. a base class the application meaningfully extends, or a runtime/framework the application is fundamentally built around) — otherwise leave imported library internals out entirely rather than diagram them on equal footing with the real components. If any of the components are web/UI components assembled from multiple artifacts (e.g. a template, a stylesheet, and a logic file, as with an Angular, Vue, or React component), collapse each one into a single node representing that component as a whole — never split it into separate nodes for its markup, styling, and logic, and never include a purely presentational/styling artifact as its own node.
